@seth_fin Lets go Seth! There is deteriorating breath in the markets right now. This is the troubling sign that markets will remain choppy and weak. 

However, once clarity comes into the markets, I expect us to hold and rally. Balanced portfolios in my opinion are the best plays right now. 

If high growth, I would stick to strong names with actual growing revenues, cash flows, etc. If you have moonshot plays, I would size that appropriately as well. Nothing sized too huge that will kill your entire account during this time.

@UnderseaLorien Partly! more money in the system DOES push asset prices up. but the CAPE already adjusts for this. It uses real (inflation adjusted) earnings over 10 years, so it filters out the effect of the dollar losing purchasing power.

what it doesn't fully account for is structural shifts like stock buybacks, passive indexing, and the concentration of earnings in mega cap tech. Apple, Microsoft, Nvidia, and Google earn margins that companies in the 1950s could never have imagined so some of the premium is justified 

The question is how much. at 42x you're paying for PEFECTION. If earnings growth slows even slightly, the multiple has a long way to compress which is the scary part haha

@joecdean11 Definitely right Joe! The CAPE was around 27x before the 2008 crash. Not even in the top quartile historically. The GFC wasn't a valuation problem, it was a credit and leverage problem. Housing, banks, and derivatives blew up regardless of what stocks were priced at.

The CAPE doesn't predict crashes. It predicts long term forward returns. High CAPE = lower returns over the next
decade. Low CAPE = higher returns. It's a valuation thermometer, not a timing tool.

